= Abstract

This is an example dissertation produced by the `cardinal-su-thesis` template.
Every section you would expect in a Stanford dissertation is present, and every
feature the template provides is exercised somewhere in the text, so that this
compiled document doubles as a working reference. Replace this content with your
own.

An abstract in the body of the dissertation has no maximum length. Note that
the abstract you paste into the Axess submission form is a separate, plain-text
abstract limited to 5,000 characters, with no Markdown or LaTeX markup.

The remaining preliminary pages demonstrate the acknowledgements and preface,
both optional. After those come the table of contents, a list of figures, and a
list of tables, and then the main text.

= Acknowledgements

Acknowledgements, a preface, and a dedication are all optional, and may appear
in any combination after the abstract. The Registrar asks that you use
discretion when naming private individuals here, to avoid inadvertently
disclosing identities or affecting anyone's privacy.

This section also shows that long runs of ordinary prose flow correctly at the
template's default line spacing of one-and-a-half. Body text is justified with
a first-line indent on every paragraph except the first of a section, matching
the convention used for the rest of the manuscript.

= Preface

A preface is the conventional place to explain how the work came about, to note
which chapters correspond to published papers, and to identify co-authors and
their contributions. If any chapter of your dissertation has been published or
co-authored, the Registrar expects that relationship to be stated.

Delete any of these three sections you do not need. None of them is required.
